content.text:
No need to read anything — I can trace this purely from the `MergeService` code we already read.

The second loop in `merge()` handles exactly this:

```java
// first loop: walks stored sections
for (String section : storedSections.keySet()) { ... }

// second loop: catches sections that only exist in the follow-up
for (String section : followUpSections.keySet()) {
    if (!mergedSections.containsKey(section)) {
        mergedSections.put(section, mergeSection(Map.of(), followUpSections.get(section)));
    }
}
```

When `hospital` is new, `mergeSection` is called with an **empty map** as stored fields. Inside `mergeSection`:

- The first loop (over stored fields) does nothing — stored is empty
- The second loop (over follow-up fields) runs and marks every field as `"new"` since none exist in stored

So the response would look like:

```json
"hospital": {
  "hospital_name": { "value": "AIIMS Delhi", "confidence": 0.91, "status": "new" },
  "ward":          { "value": "Cardiology",  "confidence": 0.85, "status": "new" }
}
```

And it persists correctly — the next follow-up would then diff against these fields normally.

**The code handles this correctly out of the box.** No gaps here.

